WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.132
Hello, and welcome to our conference call,
MBA Admissions Insights,

00:00:10.132 --> 00:00:12.200
Getting Ready to Apply.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.840
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.300
My name is Allison Davis.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.500
I'm associate director in
the MBA admissions office.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.400
I'm joined by my colleague Luke Pena,
director of MBA admissions.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:22.900
Hi everyone.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.670
&gt;&gt; In the next 30 minutes
we'll be talking about

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.250
what to keep in mind as you consider
applying to business school.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:34.510
We'll start by debunking
a few common misconceptions.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.980
Then we'll discuss some questions that
you may find worthwhile to ask yourself

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.080
at this stage in the process.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.500
With some tips on how to
go about answering them.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.560
We'll wrap up by answering some of
the questions submitted by you in advance.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.360
So let's get started, Luke,
by debunking a few myths.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.860
First myth, people with my background
don't go to business school.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.450
&gt;&gt; Hm, sure I hear this a lot.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.350
I can debunk this myth.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.700
Look, once upon a time the profiles
of business school students,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.250
I do believe it was very narrow.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.770
But management education has evolved
a lot in the last few decades and

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.040
the last few years.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.500
So now you see many business schools
focusing much more broadly on

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.320
the idea of developing leadership.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.870
And so this means aspiring managers
are now recognizing good leadership.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.960
This is needed across all industries and
across all companies,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.850
not just along the so
called traditional backgrounds.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:32.580
In fact those of you who with academic and

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.560
professional backgrounds where
leadership training is less common

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.710
actually may have the most to gain from
an MBA and the most to contribute.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:45.630
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, the potential for learning
and for impact with the background that

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.470
might not be called traditional
to specially pronounced.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:53.260
So at Stanford, we don't have a preference
for a certain academic background or

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.270
a certain professional profile.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:59.210
We take the position that diversity
of experience as immensely and

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.780
it's integral to your experience.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.830
So that means there's
not one right profile.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:06.120
There's also not a correct
socioeconomic profile and

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.470
I know that's often a great concern, and
many of you might worry about the cost,

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:13.710
and we work to help offset this cost
with need-based financial aid programs.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.620
So, I believe that regardless of your
background, if you're excited to learn and

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.370
grow, if you want to help others learn and
grow,

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:22.750
if you're passionate about
leading organizations and

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.850
changing the world in meaningful ways,
you should consider a Stanford MBA.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.860
Here's a common myth.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.155
I'm too old to pursue an MBA or
I'm too young to pursue an MBA.

00:02:33.155 --> 00:02:34.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Sure.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.690
Well, let me dispel
with the notion of age.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.010
We don't consider your age
in this process at all.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.330
We think about this as career stage.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:46.230
And look, people at all different
career stages can benefit from an MBA.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.550
Now, acknowledge the reasons and the
objectives for pursuing an MBA can differ

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.600
a lot based on where you were on your
career and that's great and that's fine.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.590
I tend to think it's exciting to be
part of community like Stanford where

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.740
there's a range of objectives and
goals but

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:05.080
everybody wants to create meaningful
impact and we sometimes call it an insane

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.100
level of greatness and
we acknowledge that everyone defines that

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:13.090
greatness is a different way including
based on where you are in your career.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.690
So I tend to think of the MBA
as an investment, right,

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.450
it's an investment in yourself and
in the communities in which you operate.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.400
And there's probably never a wrong
time to invest in your own

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:23.480
learning and development.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.040
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.410
It's never too early in your career
to invest in being a better and

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.140
more self aware leader.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.270
On the flip side,

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:34.380
you're never too experienced to
invest in your leadership ability.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.160
So at Stanford, we like having this range
of experience in the class where you can

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.960
learn from others who have different
profiles, different levels of experience.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.670
So regardless of the experience level,
if you recognize the value of this

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.570
investment, and investing in
your leadership ability, again,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.550
I'd encourage you to
consider the Stanford MBA.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:57.410
&gt;&gt; Another myth, an MBA would narrow my
professional options, not broaden them.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:02.040
&gt;&gt; Hm, well, this is a myth that
I think mirrors the first myth.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.640
That we talked about, which was about
a narrow set of pre-MBA backgrounds.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:06.790
This is the flip side.

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.420
This is that there
are narrow post-MBA paths.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.230
So let me dispel this myth.

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:15.150
Remember, we talked about
management education evolving.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:19.770
It's become largely industry and
company agnostic,

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:23.560
and its focused on developing better and
more impactful leaders.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:27.990
So at Stanford we focused on things
like general management curriculum.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.480
We talk about innovative thinking,

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.840
we focus on personal leadership
development and global understanding.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.200
These are all concepts
that are applicable across

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.810
a wide variety of professional paths.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:44.510
So this means you can apply
this tool kit of skills and

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:49.800
experience, you can apply it to whatever
noble pursuit has meaning for you.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:53.020
&gt;&gt; When you say that another way
the MBA broadens your options is

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:53.930
through the community?

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.070
&gt;&gt; Sure, sure that's a great point.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:01.010
You have a diverse community of
classmates and alums and faculty, and

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:06.530
these individuals are providing you
exposure to a wide variety of career path.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:10.650
So, many of these career paths are ones
you may have not encountered before.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:14.190
So, at Stanford you're given these
opportunities for exploration, and

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:17.500
access to our community that
encourages exploration.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.400
So, my belief is that Stanford MBA
actually positions you for

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.400
a much broader set.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.850
Professional options.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.940
It also imparts you with
the transferable skills

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.350
that will allow you to change
paths all throughout your career.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.240
&gt;&gt; Okay last myth.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.860
It's best to know what my post MBA goals
are before I go to business school.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:38.730
&gt;&gt; Mm yeah.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:42.160
Well this builds on our
discussion at the last myth.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:48.160
So MBA at a place like Stanford we talked
about how this broadens opportunities for

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:49.460
professional options.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.590
So what this means is that in business
school it is actually a great setting,

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:58.460
maybe even the ideal setting,
to exercise your curiosity,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.160
to explore different career paths.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:06.340
And it's ideal here, because you can
explore and the downside risk is low.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.880
You can use your time here as a student
to do things like pursue an internship in

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:14.800
a completely different field, or
shadow a practitioner from industry.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.250
Or even just schedule things like coffee
chats or lunch talks to explore and

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.450
satisfy this curiosity.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:24.430
And students who explore
sometimes find their goals change

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:26.240
even as they're exploring, don't they?

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.430
&gt;&gt; Absolutely,
you might be surprised to hear that it's

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:33.250
actually 75% of our students here
that switch careers after the MBA,

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:37.910
and this is in large part because of this
exposure to classmates, and alums, and

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:42.530
the faculty and the visiting speakers that
Give you these opportunities to explore.

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:48.100
And so you may even have an idea of goals
coming in, and that may change and evolve

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.560
as you interact with all these different
kinds of people in the community.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.070
So I will say if you are somebody
who has very clear end goals,

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:56.720
you are very welcome at Stanford.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.630
But you are equally
welcome if your reason for

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:02.150
pursuing an MBA at
Stanford is to explore and

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:04.700
of course we'll be very interested in
hearing about the ways you do that.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:07.350
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Luke.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:11.330
Next let's talk about questions
that you should be asking yourself

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.300
as you're getting ready to apply.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.970
In fact answering these questions for

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:20.380
yourself now can probably save you
time later in the application process.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:25.495
So one thing to ask yourself that's useful
is, is an MBA the right degree for me?

00:07:25.495 --> 00:07:27.480
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, yeah.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.000
Well let me start making an assumption and

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.250
this assumption is that if
you're listening right now.

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:34.340
You are interested in growning,

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:38.290
you are interested in improving both
on a professional and a personal level.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:39.590
So I make that assumption.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:42.230
So to answer this question
is the MBA right for me,

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.600
you have to ask yourself
how do you want to grow?

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.170
How do you want to improve and
evolve and change?

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.950
And so there are lots of ways to do this.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:52.876
And I will acknowledge some
of the different paths.

00:07:52.876 --> 00:07:56.222
You know, some of you are deciding MBA or
stay in the job?

00:07:56.222 --> 00:07:57.656
Graduate degree or stay in the job?

00:07:57.656 --> 00:08:01.070
And maybe your current job gives you
opportunities to grow and develop.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.936
Some of you are deciding which kind
of a graduate degree to pursue.

00:08:04.936 --> 00:08:09.370
There are graduate degrees, other
professional degrees, I acknowledge that.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:13.330
Some of you have decided that management
education is right for you, but

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:14.790
there are different formats.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:18.440
There is part time, there is executive
programs, there's online programs and

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:22.230
certificate programs, and
these all have various pros and cons.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.810
But even at Stanford,
we have different programs like Ignite,

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:29.970
which is a four-week program, sometimes
actually a seven to nine-week program for

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:33.260
working professionals both on campus and
around the world.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.150
We have a handful of certificates.

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:38.750
There's an innovation and
entrepreneurship certificate online.

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:41.920
There's a LEAD certificate online,
which stands for Learn,

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.500
Engage, Accelerate, Disrupt.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.040
We also have an MSX program.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.860
This is a 12 month program, for

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.770
those of you with at least
eight years of experience.

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:54.860
So there are so many options out there,
I acknowledge, that will help you grow and

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:55.390
improve.

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:59.470
So it's worth while to pause and
evaluate all of these, and

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:02.550
see which one is really the right fit for
growth and improvement for you.

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:05.170
&gt;&gt; So,
relative to all those other options,

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:08.260
what does an MBA offer would you say?

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:11.660
&gt;&gt; Well, I'm biased of course, but
I'm bullish on the MBA, and that's for

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:12.660
two reasons.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:17.020
The first is the incredible
versatility of this degree.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:21.990
As I mentioned, the focus of an MBA
here at Stanford, it's not on a narrow

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:27.730
specific career path, it's not on
a narrowly defined set of skills.

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.420
It's actually on broad
applicable concepts.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:35.380
So, innovative thinking, the personal
leadership development, self awareness,

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.540
interpersonal experiences,
understanding and

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:40.725
navigating the global
landscape around you.

00:09:40.725 --> 00:09:45.170
These are all very valuable skills
in a very versatile sense, and

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:49.170
so this is a skill set that actually
makes you a very versatile professional.

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:52.730
&gt;&gt; So, the MBA isn't just for
people pursuing careers in business?

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:53.980
&gt;&gt; No, not at all.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:56.980
These skills are applicable all
across different career paths,

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.480
across different cultures.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:03.430
And frankly, very appropriate for
a world that's changing rapidly around us.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:07.360
So, the diversity of the skill
set is broad and versatile.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.700
The community is also very broad.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:13.790
And so, this is a community that provides
perspectives that are much more varied

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:18.160
than you often find in the workplace,
or in other graduate degree programs.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:20.930
Again, people are coming from
all these different backgrounds.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.800
And that gives you
a learning environment and

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.710
the laboratory to expand your
perspective and abilities.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:31.820
So that's one reason, the second reason
why I'm bullish on the MBA, it's the idea

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:36.180
of the curation of all of these
distinct experiences coming together.

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:40.620
So, sometimes people ask me why
would I even bother with the MBA?

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:42.610
And that's a fair question.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:47.130
So in my opinion,
I believe it's impossible to curate,

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:52.180
all by yourself, all of the experiences
that an MBA simultaneously offers.

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:56.370
Can you replicate classroom learning
with the faculty that we have here?

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:58.020
Right.
Can you replicate the emphasis on

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:02.410
innovation, the immersive
opportunities to practice leadership,

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:03.790
the global experiences.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:06.500
Can you curate this all on your own?

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:11.470
Can you assemble 400 plus people who are
some of the most amazing and inspiring and

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:14.506
ambitious future leaders to make
that your own support network?

00:11:14.506 --> 00:11:18.940
But if you can do all this on your own,
then maybe you don't need the MBA, but

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:23.485
if you recognize value in immersing
yourself in environmental where all of

00:11:23.485 --> 00:11:27.990
this comes together, then I encourage you,
take a good long look at a Stanford MBA.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.740
Now, I should mention one other thing.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:35.030
Some of you are interested in Management
Education and a Business Degree.

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:38.660
You're also interested in studying
something else simultaneously.

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:43.330
And this is a really exciting and
growing area in business and MBA schools.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:45.360
It's the idea of doing
a joint dual degree.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:46.640
And so, many schools,

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.680
Stanford among them, offer the opportunity
to study business and something else.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:55.570
And so, if that applies to you,
you can pursue that.

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:58.800
We'll actually talk more about joint
dual degrees in another one of these

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.640
discussions coming in the next few months.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:01.960
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Luke.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:05.450
Another question, when is the right
time for me to go back to school?

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:09.980
&gt;&gt; Well, let me address
an important distinction here so

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:12.580
there are different times
to apply to school.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:16.630
If, let's say you're applying to
Stanford as a college senior, or

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:20.540
as a current graduate student who has
continued straight through from undergrad.

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:22.020
You have the option of applying for

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:27.070
admission, and if you're admitted,
deferring your admission to a future year.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:29.350
So, similarly,
if you're already working, but

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:32.430
you are interested in applying
to a joint or dual degree,

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:36.610
you may also defer your admission
to pursue that other degree first.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:39.500
So those of you that fall
into those groups have

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:41.720
some flexibility about when you start.

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:45.440
Now, if you're already working and
you're not applying for a joint or

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:49.800
a dual degree, we do expect you to apply
in the year when you plan to enroll.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:53.850
So for those of you who are in this
position, this is a very important and

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:55.930
a very timely question to ask.

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:59.130
And the short and simple answer is,
when you're ready.

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.120
You go back to school when you're ready.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:04.520
And, you know, remember our
dispelling of the myth earlier that

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:07.010
you could have too little experience or
too much experience.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:08.150
That's not true.

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:13.150
This frees you up to apply at that point
in your career when you are truly ready.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:16.760
&gt;&gt; So the obvious question is,
how would you know if you're ready?

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:17.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
Sure.

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:21.650
Well, I believe the right time
is when you are ready for

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:24.760
change, when you're ready for
some sort of change.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:27.930
Now, that is defined different ways for
each of you.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:31.980
All right, for some of you, that might
be changing the very work that you do.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:33.300
You want to change industries.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:37.420
You want to change the very nature
of the work that you're doing.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:41.652
Others of you may like the industry you're
in, or the company you work for, but you

00:13:41.652 --> 00:13:45.100
want to change your approach, and how you
think about the work that you're doing.

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:49.530
So there are different ways to define
this, but I believe that, the time to know

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:53.770
when you're ready, it's when you recognize
this opportunity to advance your passions.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.860
To advance your interests by making
this investments in yourself, and

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:58.109
the impact you have on your community.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:02.710
&gt;&gt; Isn't there a cost, though,
to interrupting your career?

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:06.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure, in fact,
I think of two costs.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:08.920
The first cost is a financial cost.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:13.340
Right now, business school is not only
an investment in yourself, in your skills,

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:16.410
it's also a literal financial investment,
and

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.870
I acknowledge you spend
money to come to school.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:22.350
But I also hope that you don't
consider that a deterrent.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:26.860
And so, we're fortunate at Stanford to
have a very generous need-based financial

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:31.250
aid program, and this helps fund students
based on your financial need to attend,

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:34.780
so we strive to eliminate
that as a barrier for study.

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:39.330
Now, the second cost is opportunity
cost of stepping away from your career.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:43.100
And so, for a full time MBA like Stanford,
that is stepping away for 2 years,

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:47.080
perhaps even longer if you are pursuing
one of the joint dual degree programs.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:49.910
And so there are professional
considerations, there are personal.

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:51.300
It's considerations.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:55.720
Are you moving a family,
are you delaying some

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:01.340
other goal that's involve in your personal
life to come and study at business school?

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:03.110
I acknowledge all of these things.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:07.640
So, when you think about this cause
I want to return to the framework of

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:11.200
business school as an investment,
it's an investment in yourself.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:15.280
It's an investment in your future, and
it's an investment in the communities, and

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:18.270
the impact that you'll have within them.

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:22.120
So, those of you who have
experience with investing no,

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.760
with an investment
there's an up front cost.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:27.430
And, as with any worthwhile investment,

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:30.306
there are also rewards
that justify that cost.

00:15:30.306 --> 00:15:34.390
And so, you know, the rewards here
from an MBA and from a Stanford MBA,

00:15:34.390 --> 00:15:38.770
they may be financial, but I actually
believe the more valuable rewards

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:43.140
manifest themselves in the form of
a career impact, a life of meaning.

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:45.998
So I say think not just
about the short term cost,

00:15:45.998 --> 00:15:50.763
think about the long-term rewards that
pay dividends for the rest of your life.

00:15:50.763 --> 00:15:54.691
And so when you're ready, regardless of
your career stage, when you're ready to

00:15:54.691 --> 00:15:58.617
make this investment that does have the
short-term cost, but pays the long-term

00:15:58.617 --> 00:16:02.347
dividends in impact and meaning,
that's when you know it's the right time.

00:16:02.347 --> 00:16:06.105
Third question,
is this the right school for me?

00:16:06.105 --> 00:16:08.761
&gt;&gt; Hm, there's so
many schools around the world, and so

00:16:08.761 --> 00:16:11.033
many of them offering
management education.

00:16:11.033 --> 00:16:15.481
And sometimes this can feel like a curse,
because there's an abundance of options,

00:16:15.481 --> 00:16:18.444
but I actually encourage you
to think of it as a blessing,

00:16:18.444 --> 00:16:20.900
because each school is different.

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:24.150
And that means each one has
a different value proposition.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:28.860
Now, there's a myth that we didn't dispel
earlier, but I want to dispel now.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:34.580
That myth is that there is some universal
ranked order of schools and that a higher

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:39.170
ranked school is inherently better than
a lower ranked school for all of you.

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:41.170
That's simply not true.

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:45.290
Each one of you listening has a different
set of schools that are best for you,

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:49.980
and this is based on a widely
varying set of personal criteria.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:53.380
So in this sense do
the research on the schools and

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:57.710
do that research not based on some list or
what somebody else says is right.

00:16:57.710 --> 00:17:00.440
Apply your criteria that's important.

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.890
So for some of you,
you'll care about location.

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:05.520
Some of you will care about
the culture of the school.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:08.200
Some of you will care about how
are you taught in the class,

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:09.760
what are the teaching methods?

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.140
Some of you care about,
is it a general management program or

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:16.790
other concentrations and majors that
allow you to very deeply specialize?

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:19.150
So pay attention to the criteria, but

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:23.667
pay attention in regards to your
specific set of interests and desires.

00:17:23.667 --> 00:17:26.090
&gt;&gt; What would you say
differentiates Stanford?

00:17:26.090 --> 00:17:30.390
&gt;&gt; Hm, well, I think of this in
three frames, in three elements, and

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:31.560
they're progressively deeper.

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:35.770
So the first element is
programmatic features.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:38.270
Okay so,
these are things like location, right?

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:41.980
So we're in California and
this is a place where there's enthusiasm

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:44.520
about innovation and
exploring the unknown.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:46.350
I think about the structure
of the program.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.600
So ours is a general
management curriculum.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:53.410
So we focus on broad applicable skills
that are transferable across industries.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:56.370
All right, we focus on things like
experiential leadership rather than

00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:58.490
strictly theoretical instruction.

00:17:58.490 --> 00:17:59.850
In fact the classroom instruction.

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:04.070
It's a combination of both research,
faculty and practitioners.

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:06.060
So these are the programmatic elements.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:09.790
If you go one level deeper,
you look at the people in a community.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:11.460
So at Stanford we have a small class.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:13.690
It's just over 400 in each class.

00:18:13.690 --> 00:18:17.050
And the students get to know each other
not just as classmates but as friends and

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:20.090
as people that they can count
on to support one another.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:23.060
All right,
it's a feedback dominated culture.

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:27.000
So we encourage students to practice
giving each other feedback and

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:30.467
to develop each others leadership
through this practice.

00:18:30.467 --> 00:18:33.667
Now the alumni community, as a result
of the small, tight knit community,

00:18:33.667 --> 00:18:36.040
it's very strong, it's very responsive.

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:40.200
But then you go even one level deeper and
you arrive at the values.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:44.300
And so the values of a place like Stanford
focus on personal transformation.

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:46.530
They focus on self awareness.

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:50.210
And ultimately focus on
values based decision making.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:53.690
And making decisions based on what
has meaning and impact for you.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:58.240
So this, to me, suggests a really powerful
insight about the values at Stanford.

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:02.900
When you come here and
you graduate we hope that your pursuit and

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:06.620
your goal is not success, success is fine.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.010
It's a good thing.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:13.700
It's fine to want, but we hope that's not
the primary goal because it's superficial.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:17.310
It's temporary, and
time's completely out of our control.

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:20.260
So if this is your goal,
you may or may not find it.

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:24.732
So instead, we encourage you to position,
to transform and

00:19:24.732 --> 00:19:29.042
think of the goal as happiness and
meaning, and purpose.

00:19:29.042 --> 00:19:32.690
And so, these are noble pursuits that
you can, in fact, attain and achieve.

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:35.680
So if this is your pursuit,
chances are you'll find it, and

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:38.190
chances are you're more likely
to find success as a byproduct.

00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:42.420
&gt;&gt; Sounds all pretty aspirational.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:46.800
What tactical steps can our listeners
take to determine if Stanford or

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:48.500
another school is right for them?

00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:50.730
&gt;&gt; Sure, two suggestions.

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:52.370
The first, engage with us.

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:55.110
We've got lots of
opportunities to engage with

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:58.210
the Stanford community as well as
other business school communities.

00:19:58.210 --> 00:20:01.230
So if you're so inclined,
come and see us on campus.

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:02.460
If you can't visit campus,

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:06.030
attend one of the events around
the world that we put on.

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:07.250
Check us out online.

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:08.880
We have more of these
online events coming.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:12.470
We've got some great videos online,
look at student profiles.

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:16.680
So we put out a lot of content for
you to understand the school.

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:19.830
Now, this is a lot of data and
information from the school but

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:21.610
it can be one directional.

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:23.780
So, you also have to self access.

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:27.730
You have to take action in response
to learning about the school and

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:31.210
self access to make sure the community
is aligned with your own values.

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:36.470
And I think one simple trick for this is
look at what the school is asking you.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:40.560
For example, in the application essays,
sketch out some light responses and

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.410
see what reaction you have.

00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:45.940
If you get excited about that,
that tells you one thing.

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:47.770
And if you have a different reaction,

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:50.670
that may tell you another thing
about your fit with the school.

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:54.830
&gt;&gt; Great, Luke, let's move on now
to answering audience questions.

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:57.640
Thanks to so many of you who
submitted questions in advance.

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:03.630
Okay, what are some things we
can do three to six months prior

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:07.640
to submitting an application
to a full time MBA program?

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:11.450
Three to six months,
this is a great time for reflection.

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:15.370
I think sometimes the temptation is to
think of the application as just a series

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:18.990
of check boxes and steps that you
have to go through to be a candidate.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:23.030
But actually, reflection goes a long way
towards helping you know if this is truly

00:21:23.030 --> 00:21:24.770
the right path for you.

00:21:24.770 --> 00:21:29.180
So once you've decided that this
is the right path, plan it.

00:21:29.180 --> 00:21:30.580
Now set, put together a plan.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:32.690
Look at the deadline for
when you want to apply and

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:34.670
work backwards from that deadline.

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:37.302
So that you prepare and
give yourself enough time.

00:21:37.302 --> 00:21:40.820
I also encourage you to
identify the resources

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:42.950
that will help you best for preparing.

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:47.030
And so resources look like they
manifest in a lot of different ways.

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:50.190
But I think maybe the most important
is get the people around you who

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.520
will be your support network, who can
give you feedback on your application and

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:56.590
who can give you guidance on preparing for
this big step.

00:21:56.590 --> 00:22:00.440
And then finally as I mentioned,
check in with yourself along the way.

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.090
Self assess, how are you feeling?

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:05.610
How are you responding as you're
moving through this process?

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:06.380
Are you excited?

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:06.950
Are you not?

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:08.050
Are you dreading it?

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:11.800
This is valuable feedback for yourself
that you should take into consideration.

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:16.160
&gt;&gt; This next question is
about application rounds.

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:20.560
I've heard that admissions rates differ
between different application rounds.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:24.710
How should this factor into our decision
about which round to apply for?

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:27.900
&gt;&gt; Well,
you should apply when you are ready, and

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:29.580
when your application is complete.

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:31.470
Now I think its important to know,

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:36.120
we do not enter a round with a quota for
admission offers.

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:40.160
We don't go into a round with
a fixed admission rate in mind.

00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:43.450
The number of admission offers in a round
depends on the number of qualified

00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:47.400
candidates relative to the pool, and
that changes from one round to the next,

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:50.950
from one year to the next, and
it's not something we predict.

00:22:50.950 --> 00:22:52.970
So apply when you're ready.

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:57.500
I do think it's wise to apply as early
as your application is complete.

00:22:57.500 --> 00:23:01.060
Round three has historically been
a very competitive round for us.

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:05.830
And in the last few years, we've received
more applications in round two, and

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:08.040
that's made that round more competitive.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:11.219
So, on the margin, I would say,
apply as early as you can,

00:23:11.219 --> 00:23:14.149
but, the most important factor
is that you should feel

00:23:14.149 --> 00:23:17.289
your application is complete and
you are ready to submit.

00:23:18.670 --> 00:23:22.420
&gt;&gt; Luke, what advice do you have for more
traditional applicants who already have

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:25.730
some exposure to the MBA
application process?

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:28.420
What should they focus
on in their preparation?

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:30.300
&gt;&gt; Well, the focus should be on yourself.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.220
And what do I mean by that?

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:35.685
You want to really,
truly examine why this path,

00:23:35.685 --> 00:23:39.741
this MBA, this timing, and
this school is right for you.

00:23:39.741 --> 00:23:41.595
And I think this is
a specially critical for

00:23:41.595 --> 00:23:44.791
those of you where there are many
candidates applying from your company or

00:23:44.791 --> 00:23:48.550
from your industry because there is
a temptation to fall under group think.

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:50.485
Right?
Everybody else is applying to the school

00:23:50.485 --> 00:23:53.095
or applying at this time in their
career so yo you should too.

00:23:53.095 --> 00:23:58.035
Now, let me challenge that, you want to
resist that temptation, I think you also

00:23:58.035 --> 00:24:02.482
have to work to resist the temptation
to try to differentiate yourself.

00:24:02.482 --> 00:24:06.382
And again, you see yourself
wanting to position yourself and

00:24:06.382 --> 00:24:11.000
compare yourself to the others
that you know who are applying.

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:12.270
I say, let go of that.

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:13.910
It's hard, but let go of that.

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:17.610
Those who try hardest to stand out in this
process are usually the ones who end up

00:24:17.610 --> 00:24:19.000
standing out the least.

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:21.910
So instead of trying to be different,
just be yourself.

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:26.020
And that's the best way to be a great
candidate, and let that sense of self and

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:27.800
identity really emerge.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:32.190
&gt;&gt; Here's a question from an international
student who asks, if I've already visited

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:36.620
the campus and attended a class, what are
some of the other opportunities I should

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:41.000
be looking out for which will help me
understand the Stanford culture better?

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.320
&gt;&gt; Hm, I like that idea of culture.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:47.300
Culture is such a significant part of
the business school experience it's

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:50.190
certainly a differentiating
factor among business schools.

00:24:50.190 --> 00:24:52.450
And so this is an important
thing to pay attention to.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:57.320
Now, culture isn't manufactured
in the abstract by a school.

00:24:57.320 --> 00:25:01.970
No, it forms and emerges from the people
who are part of that community.

00:25:01.970 --> 00:25:05.600
So to really understand a culture,
you have to understand the people.

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:06.440
What do they value?

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:07.690
What drives them?

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:09.760
What gives them passion and meaning.

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:15.560
And so this actually allows me to lump
in another question that we received.

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:19.690
And that question is, is there a way to
connect with Standford GSB students or

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:21.210
alumni as we prepare to apply?

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:24.310
And this is in the spirit of wanting
to get to know that culture.

00:25:24.310 --> 00:25:25.430
Absolutely.

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:29.860
We want you to know our community,
to know our students and alums, and so

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:33.060
we have a lot of ways
that you can do this.

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:37.140
Right, if you do visit campus, we have
students here who speak at our sessions,

00:25:37.140 --> 00:25:38.780
especially when school is in session.

00:25:38.780 --> 00:25:42.700
And we have class visits, but
even if you've already visited campus, or

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:46.450
you can't visit campus, we're going out in
the next few months all over the world and

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:49.900
hosting events in the large
metropolitan areas of the world.

00:25:49.900 --> 00:25:52.480
We feature alumni and
students at those events and so

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:54.630
it is a way to directly engage.

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.560
We have a lot of great
information on the web,

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:59.790
there are social media
profiles of students,

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:03.070
we have a week in the life series where
students highlight what a week in their

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:08.010
life looks like, we've got some great
new videos on our YouTube channel.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:11.050
Where students talk about what
the experience is like at the school.

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:13.740
And so you've got faculty videos,
visiting speaker videos, so

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:17.590
there's some great content online
where you can understand the culture.

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:20.670
There's also these events where you can
interact with students and alums directly.

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:26.770
&gt;&gt; Does admissions see and
care if an applicant has visited campus?

00:26:26.770 --> 00:26:29.170
&gt;&gt; We're delighted to
have you visit campus.

00:26:29.170 --> 00:26:31.220
We're delighted to offer
you these opportunities.

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:35.290
We have information sessions Monday and
Friday all throughout the year.

00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:37.610
We have class visits
during three quarters.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:40.880
The fall, the winter, and
the spring when classes are in session.

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.690
We even have larger visit programs.

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:43.840
These are coming this September.

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:47.040
So things like mini voices for
diverse perspectives.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:50.370
We have an XX factor event for
women who want to change the world.

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:52.490
We've got a visit day which
is a general visit event,

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:54.230
and these are day long events.

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:57.400
Now, we have these opportunities but
I want to be very clear,

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:01.200
we offer these to help you understand
the Stanford experience and

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:05.060
culture and many applicants find this to
be a helpful step in preparation, but

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:08.030
we do not weigh them in
our evaluation process.

00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:10.280
It's very important to preserve
fairness and equity, and

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:14.150
we understand that some of you have the
opportunity to visit, some of you do not,

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:17.830
and so it's not an advantage or
a disadvantage in the evaluation process.

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:18.870
It's for your research.

00:27:20.350 --> 00:27:23.630
&gt;&gt; All right, Luke,
we're almost out of time.

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:26.030
If you didn't get your
question answered today,

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:30.850
feel free to contact our office by
email or phone to answer your question.

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:34.820
Luke, before we go, any words of
advice for our listeners today?

00:27:36.588 --> 00:27:40.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've been careful not to talk
too much about the application itself.

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:42.370
It is important to us, but

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:46.350
we wanted to focus today first on
how to get ready to apply, and

00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:51.090
we will talk about the application in
a future discussion in this series.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:55.440
Now that being said, our first essay
question is a question that I believe is

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:59.010
worth considering not just when
completing the application but

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:03.180
also when you're deciding why to apply,
when to apply, where to apply.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:07.520
And that first question is,
what matters most to you and why?

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:10.960
Okay, so we believe that
the purpose of a Stanford MBA

00:28:10.960 --> 00:28:15.380
is to position you to change lives,
change organizations, to change the world.

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:19.800
But in order to do that, you have to
first examine how you might change, and

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:21.690
you might grow, and you might evolve.

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:25.280
And in order to do that,
you have to know and understand yourself.

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:29.420
So before you pick a degree, before
you pick the year or round to apply,

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:33.460
before you pick the schools to apply to,
you owe it to yourself to really,

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:38.790
truly examine who you are, examine what
matters to you, and examine what about

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:42.460
your answer drives and motivates you to
take the step in your career in your life.

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:46.800
If you do that work of self-examination up
front, you will get a better applicant,

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:50.460
you'll be a happier student, and
you'll be a more impactful alum.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.330
&gt;&gt; Great advice Lou, thank you.

00:28:52.330 --> 00:28:54.370
And thank you all for joining us.

00:28:54.370 --> 00:28:59.250
There are three more MBA admissions
insight conference calls coming up,

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:02.190
including, preparing for
your letters of reference and

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:07.570
essays on July 12th,
being a great applicant on August 10th and

00:29:07.570 --> 00:29:11.720
considering a joint or
dual degree on August 31st.

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.370
You can sign up for these on our website.

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:16.970
An audio recording of each
of these conference calls,

00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:22.010
including the one today, will be posted
on our website in the next few weeks.

00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:25.730
The current application is
now available on our website

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:30.720
there're three application deadlines
September 21, January 10, and April 5.

00:29:30.720 --> 00:29:35.110
As Luke said earlier, there're lots of
ways to learn more about our program and

00:29:35.110 --> 00:29:36.730
the admission process.

00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:39.510
On our website you'll find
videos of webinars and

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:43.950
a calendar of events offered both
on campus and around the world.

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:48.390
We look forward to meeting you online or
in person.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:49.410
Goodbye for now.

